1. [THEOREM] Reciprocal Identity for Square Root: $\frac{\sqrt{x}}{x} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}$
Module: Mathlib.Data.Real.Sqrt
Name: Real.sqrt_div_self'
Description: For any real number $x$, the quotient of the square root of $x$ divided by $x$ equals the reciprocal of the square root of $x$, i.e., $\frac{\sqrt{x}}{x} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}$.
Signature:  : √x / x = 1 / √x

2. [THEOREM] Integral of Antitone Function on Interval Bounded by Sum Over Integer Points
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SumIntegralComparisons
Name: AntitoneOn.integral_le_sum_Ico
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be an antitone (decreasing) function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, where $a \leq b$. Then the integral of $f$ over $[a, b]$ is bounded above by the sum of $f$ evaluated at the integer points in the half-open interval $[a, b)$, i.e.,
\[
\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, dx \leq \sum_{x \in [a, b) \cap \mathbb{Z}} f(x).
\]
Signature:  (hab : a ≤ b) (hf : AntitoneOn f (Set.Icc a b)) : (∫ x in a..b, f x) ≤ ∑ x ∈ Finset.Ico a b, f x

3. [THEOREM] Integral Bounded by Sum of Dominating Function Values at Integer Points
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SumIntegralComparisons
Name: integral_le_sum_Ico_of_le
Description: Let $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$ with $a \leq b$, and let $f : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$ and $g : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be functions. Suppose that:
1. For every integer $i$ in the interval $[a, b)$ and every real $x$ in $[i, i+1)$, we have $g(x) \leq f(i)$.
2. The function $g$ is integrable on the interval $[a, b)$.

Then the integral of $g$ from $a$ to $b$ is less than or equal to the sum of $f(i)$ over integers $i$ in $[a, b)$, i.e.,
\[ \int_{a}^{b} g(x) \, dx \leq \sum_{i \in [a, b) \cap \mathbb{Z}} f(i). \]
Signature:  (hab : a ≤ b) (h : ∀ i ∈ Ico a b, ∀ x ∈ Ico (i : ℝ) (i + 1 : ℕ), g x ≤ f i) (hg : IntegrableOn g (Set.Ico a b)) :
  ∫ x in a..b, g x ≤ ∑ i ∈ Finset.Ico a b, f i

4. [THEOREM] Sum of Integer Values Bounded by Integral of Dominating Function
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SumIntegralComparisons
Name: sum_Ico_le_integral_of_le
Description: Let $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$ with $a \leq b$, and let $f : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$ and $g : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be functions. Suppose that:
1. For every integer $i$ in the interval $[a, b)$ and every real $x$ in $[i, i+1)$, we have $f(i) \leq g(x)$.
2. The function $g$ is integrable on the interval $[a, b)$.

Then the sum of $f(i)$ over integers $i$ in $[a, b)$ is less than or equal to the integral of $g$ from $a$ to $b$, i.e.,
\[ \sum_{i \in [a, b) \cap \mathbb{Z}} f(i) \leq \int_{a}^{b} g(x) \, dx. \]
Signature:  (hab : a ≤ b) (h : ∀ i ∈ Ico a b, ∀ x ∈ Ico (i : ℝ) (i + 1 : ℕ), f i ≤ g x) (hg : IntegrableOn g (Set.Ico a b)) :
  ∑ i ∈ Finset.Ico a b, f i ≤ ∫ x in a..b, g x

5. [THEOREM] Integral of Antitone Function Bounded by Sum Over Integer Points
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SumIntegralComparisons
Name: AntitoneOn.integral_le_sum
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be an antitone (decreasing) function on the interval $[x_0, x_0 + a]$. Then the integral of $f$ over $[x_0, x_0 + a]$ is bounded above by the sum of $f$ evaluated at the integer points $x_0, x_0 + 1, \ldots, x_0 + (a - 1)$, i.e.,
\[
\int_{x_0}^{x_0 + a} f(x) \, dx \leq \sum_{i=0}^{a-1} f(x_0 + i).
\]
Signature:  (hf : AntitoneOn f (Icc x₀ (x₀ + a))) : (∫ x in x₀..x₀ + a, f x) ≤ ∑ i ∈ Finset.range a, f (x₀ + i)
