   \int_{k-1}^{k} f(x)\,dx \;>\; f(k)\cdot 1 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{k}} .
   \tag{4.1}
   \]

5. **Summing over all integers.**  
   Adding (4.1) for \(k=2,3,\dots ,10000\) we obtain

   \[
   \sum_{k=2}^{10000}\frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}
   \;<\;
   \sum_{k=2}^{10000}\int_{k-1}^{k} f(x)\,dx
   \;=\;
   \int_{1}^{10000} f(x)\,dx .
   \tag{5.1}
   \]

   The equality in the middle follows because the intervals
   \([k-1,k]\) form a partition of \([1,10000]\).

6. **Evaluating the integral.**  
   An antiderivative of \(f\) is \(F(x)=2\sqrt{x}\) because
   \(\frac{d}{dx}2\sqrt{x}=2\cdot\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2}=x^{-1/2}=f(x)\).
   Therefore

   \[
   \int_{1}^{10000} f(x)\,dx
   = F(10000)-F(1)
   = 2\sqrt{10000}-2\sqrt{1}
   = 2\cdot100-2\cdot1
   = 200-2
   = 198 .
   \tag{6.1}
   \]

7. **Conclusion.**  
   Combining (5.1) and (6.1) we obtain

   \[
   \sum_{k=2}^{10000}\frac{1}{\sqrt{k}} \;<\; 198 .
   \]

   This is exactly the desired inequality.

