The integrand is \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) for \(x>0\). Its antiderivative on \((0,\infty)\) is \(F(x)=2\sqrt{x}\), since \(\frac{d}{dx}2\sqrt{x}=2\cdot\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2}=x^{-1/2}=1/\sqrt{x}\).

Evaluating the definite integral:

\[
\int_{1}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx = F(10000)-F(1)
= 2\sqrt{10000} - 2\sqrt{1}
= 2\cdot 100 - 2\cdot 1
= 200 - 2
= 198.
\]

Thus the equality \(\displaystyle\int_{1}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx = 198\) holds in \(\mathbb{R}\). The statement is therefore mathematically correct and can be proved in Lean using the standard theorem for the integral of \(x^{a}\) (with \(a=-\tfrac12\)) or by directly applying the fundamental theorem of calculus to the antiderivative \(2\sqrt{x}\).
</justification>
