\[
\sum_{k=2}^{n} f(k)\;=\;\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} f(k+1)\;<\;\int_{1}^{n} f(x)\,dx\;<\;\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} f(k)
\]

because on each sub‑interval \([k,k+1]\) the right‑endpoint value \(f(k+1)\) is a lower bound for the integral and the left‑endpoint value \(f(k)\) is an upper bound.  
Since \(f\) is strictly decreasing, the left inequality is strict.

Taking \(n=10000\) gives  

\[
\sum_{k=2}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{k}} \;<\; \int_{1}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx .
