Description: Let $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$ with $a \leq b$, and let $f : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$ and $g : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be functions. Suppose that:
1. For every integer $i$ in the interval $[a, b)$ and every real $x$ in $[i, i+1)$, we have $g(x) \leq f(i)$.
2. The function $g$ is integrable on the interval $[a, b)$.

Then the integral of $g$ from $a$ to $b$ is less than or equal to the sum of $f(i)$ over integers $i$ in $[a, b)$, i.e.,
\[ \int_{a}^{b} g(x) \, dx \leq \sum_{i \in [a, b) \cap \mathbb{Z}} f(i). \]
