   Hence we obtain  

   \[
   \tag{1}
   \sum_{i\in \text{Ico}\;1\;10000} f(i+1)\;\le\;
   \int_{1}^{10000} f(x)\,dx .
   \]

3. **Rewrite the left‑hand side as the required finite sum**  
   The set \(\text{Ico}\;1\;10000\) is the set of natural numbers
   \(\{1,2,\dots ,9999\}\).  
   Adding one to each element gives the set
   \(\{2,3,\dots ,10000\}= \text{Icc}\;2\;10000\).  
   Consequently  

   \[
   \sum_{i\in \text{Ico}\;1\;10000} f(i+1)
   \;=\;
   \sum_{k\in \text{Icc}\;2\;10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{k}} .
   \]

   Substituting this equality into (1) yields  

   \[
   \tag{2}
   \sum_{k=2}^{10000}\frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}
   \;\le\;
   \int_{1}^{10000}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{x}} .
   \]

4. **Show that the inequality in (2) is strict**  
   The integrand \(f(x)=1/\sqrt{x}\) is **strictly** positive on the whole
   interval \([1,10000]\); in particular it is positive on the sub‑interval
   \([1,2]\).  Therefore  

   \[
   \int_{1}^{2}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{x}} \;>\;0 .
   \tag{3}
   \]

   Splitting the integral at the point \(2\) we have  

   \[
   \int_{1}^{10000}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{x}}
   \;=\;
   \underbrace{\int_{1}^{2}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{x}}}_{>0}
   \;+\;
   \int_{2}^{10000}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{x}} .
   \tag{4}
   \]

   The sum on the left‑hand side of (2) contains **no** contribution that
   corresponds to the interval \([1,2]\); it only contains the terms
   \(\frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}\) for \(k\ge 2\).  Consequently the right‑hand side of
   (2) is the sum of a non‑negative quantity (the integral over \([2,10000]\))
   and the strictly positive quantity displayed in (3).  Hence the right‑hand
   side of (2) is **strictly larger** than the left‑hand side, i.e.

   \[
   \sum_{k=2}^{10000}\frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}
   \;<\;
