<justification>
The integrand \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) is continuous on the closed interval \([1,10000]\); hence it is interval‑integrable. The antiderivative of \(f\) is \(F(x)=2\sqrt{x}\), since \(\frac{d}{dx}(2\sqrt{x})=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) for every \(x>0\). By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus,

\[
\int_{1}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx = F(10000)-F(1)=2\sqrt{10000}-2\sqrt{1}.
\]

The provided hypotheses exactly give the required derivative information (`hderiv`) and the integrability (`hint`). Therefore the equality stated in the theorem is mathematically correct and can be proved from the premises.
</justification>
