<justification>
Using the standard sum formula for a finite arithmetic progression of sines,

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{n}\sin(k\theta)=\frac{\sin\!\left(\frac{n\theta}{2}\right)\sin\!\left(\frac{(n+1)\theta}{2}\right)}{\sin\!\left(\frac{\theta}{2}\right)} .
\]

Here \(\theta = 5^\circ = \frac{\pi}{36}\) and \(n = 35\).  
Hence

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{35}\sin\!\left(\frac{5k\pi}{180}\right)
= \frac{\sin\!\left(\frac{35\pi}{72}\right)\sin\!\left(\frac{36\pi}{72}\right)}
       {\sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{72}\right)}
= \frac{\sin\!\left(\frac{35\pi}{72}\right)\cdot 1}
       {\sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{72}\right)} .
\]

Since \(\cos\!\left(\frac{35\pi}{72}\right)=\sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{72}\right)\) (because
\(\frac{35\pi}{72}= \frac{\pi}{2}-\frac{\pi}{72}\)), the expression becomes

\[
\frac{\sin\!\left(\frac{35\pi}{72}\right)}{\cos\!\left(\frac{35\pi}{72}\right)}
= \tan\!\left(\frac{35\pi}{72}\right).
\]

Thus the equality stated in the theorem holds, so the theorem is mathematically correct.
</justification>
