<justification>
For the decreasing non‑negative function \(f(x)=\frac1{\sqrt{x}}\) on \([1,10000]\) we have, for each integer \(k\ge 2\),

\[
f(k)\;\le\;\int_{k-1}^{k} f(x)\,dx ,
\]

because \(f\) is antitone, so \(f(x)\ge f(k)\) for all \(x\in[k-1,k]\). Summing these inequalities from \(k=2\) to \(k=10000\) yields  

\[
\sum_{k=2}^{10000}\frac1{\sqrt{k}}
   \;\le\; \int_{1}^{10000}\frac1{\sqrt{x}}\,dx .
\]

Thus the statement  

\[
\displaystyle
\bigl(\sum_{k\in\text{Finset.Icc }2\;10000}\frac1{\sqrt{k}}\bigr)
   \le \int_{x=1}^{10000}\frac1{\sqrt{x}}\,dx
\]

is a direct consequence of the antitone hypothesis supplied in the premise. The integral evaluates to \(2(\sqrt{10000}-\sqrt{1})=198\), confirming the inequality numerically as well. Hence the theorem is mathematically correct and can be proved from the given premises.
</justification>
