<justification>
For the function \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) we have \(f>0\) and it is decreasing on \([1,10000]\) (the premise `h_antitone`).  
For a decreasing non‑negative function the integral test gives  

\[
\forall n\ge 2,\qquad 
\sum_{k=2}^{n} f(k)\;\le\;\int_{1}^{n} f(x)\,dx,
\]

because on each interval \([k-1,k]\) we have \(f(x)\ge f(k)\), hence  
\(\int_{k-1}^{k} f(x)\,dx \ge f(k)\). Summing these inequalities for \(k=2,\dots,n\) yields the desired inequality.

Taking \(n=10000\) gives exactly the statement to be proved:

\[
\sum_{k\in\text{Finset.Icc }2\;10000}\frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}
\;\le\;
\int_{1}^{10000}\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx .
\]

The additional hypothesis `h_integral_eq` (the integral equals 198) is not needed for this inequality but does not contradict it. Hence the theorem statement is mathematically valid and can be proved from the given premises.
</justification>
