1. [THEOREM] Integral of Antitone Function on Interval Bounded by Sum Over Integer Points
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SumIntegralComparisons
Name: AntitoneOn.integral_le_sum_Ico
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be an antitone (decreasing) function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, where $a \leq b$. Then the integral of $f$ over $[a, b]$ is bounded above by the sum of $f$ evaluated at the integer points in the half-open interval $[a, b)$, i.e.,
\[
\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, dx \leq \sum_{x \in [a, b) \cap \mathbb{Z}} f(x).
\]
Signature:  (hab : a ≤ b) (hf : AntitoneOn f (Set.Icc a b)) : (∫ x in a..b, f x) ≤ ∑ x ∈ Finset.Ico a b, f x

2. [THEOREM] Sum of Antitone Function at Right Endpoints is Bounded by Integral over Interval
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SumIntegralComparisons
Name: AntitoneOn.sum_le_integral_Ico
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be an antitone (decreasing) function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, where $a \leq b$. Then the sum of the values of $f$ at the right endpoints of the integer subintervals in $[a, b)$, i.e., $f(i+1)$ for each integer $i$ in the half-open interval $[a, b)$, is less than or equal to the integral of $f$ over $[a, b]$. Mathematically, this is expressed as:
\[
\sum_{i \in [a, b) \cap \mathbb{Z}} f(i+1) \leq \int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, dx.
\]
Signature:  (hab : a ≤ b) (hf : AntitoneOn f (Set.Icc a b)) : (∑ i ∈ Finset.Ico a b, f (i + 1 : ℕ)) ≤ ∫ x in a..b, f x

3. [THEOREM] Integral of Antitone Function Bounded by Sum Over Integer Points
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SumIntegralComparisons
Name: AntitoneOn.integral_le_sum
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be an antitone (decreasing) function on the interval $[x_0, x_0 + a]$. Then the integral of $f$ over $[x_0, x_0 + a]$ is bounded above by the sum of $f$ evaluated at the integer points $x_0, x_0 + 1, \ldots, x_0 + (a - 1)$, i.e.,
\[
\int_{x_0}^{x_0 + a} f(x) \, dx \leq \sum_{i=0}^{a-1} f(x_0 + i).
\]
Signature:  (hf : AntitoneOn f (Icc x₀ (x₀ + a))) : (∫ x in x₀..x₀ + a, f x) ≤ ∑ i ∈ Finset.range a, f (x₀ + i)

1. [THEOREM] Antitonicity of Right-Open Intervals: $a \leq b \implies (b, \infty) \subseteq (a, \infty)$
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Set.Monotone
Name: antitone_Ioi
Description: The function that maps each element $a$ in a preorder $\alpha$ to the right-infinite open interval $(a, \infty)$ is antitone. That is, for any $a, b \in \alpha$, if $a \leq b$, then $(b, \infty) \subseteq (a, \infty)$.
Signature:  : Antitone (Ioi : α → Set α)

2. [THEOREM] Subset Relation Between Left-Infinite Right-Closed Intervals: $(-\infty, a] \subseteq (-\infty, b] \iff a \leq b$
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Set.Basic
Name: Set.Iic_subset_Iic
Description: For any elements $a$ and $b$ in a preorder $\alpha$, the left-infinite right-closed interval $(-\infty, a]$ is a subset of $(-\infty, b]$ if and only if $a \leq b$.
Signature:  : Iic a ⊆ Iic b ↔ a ≤ b

3. [THEOREM] Right Endpoint Membership in Closed Interval: $b \in [a, b] \leftrightarrow a \leq b$
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Finset.Basic
Name: Finset.right_mem_Icc
Description: For any elements $a$ and $b$ in a locally finite order $\alpha$, the element $b$ belongs to the closed interval $\text{Icc}(a, b)$ if and only if $a \leq b$.
Signature:  : b ∈ Icc a b ↔ a ≤ b

4. [THEOREM] Subset Relation for Finite Lower-Closed Intervals: $\text{Iic}(a) \subseteq \text{Iic}(b) \iff a \leq b$
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Finset.Basic
Name: Finset.Iic_subset_Iic
Description: For any elements $a$ and $b$ in a locally finite order with finite lower-bounded intervals, the finite lower-closed interval $\text{Iic}(a) = \{x \mid x \leq a\}$ is a subset of $\text{Iic}(b) = \{x \mid x \leq b\}$ if and only if $a \leq b$.
Signature:  : Iic a ⊆ Iic b ↔ a ≤ b

5. [THEOREM] Antitonicity of the $[a, \infty)$ Interval Construction
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Set.Monotone
Name: antitone_Ici
Description: For any preorder $\alpha$, the function that maps each element $a \in \alpha$ to the left-closed right-infinite interval $[a, \infty)$ is antitone. That is, for any $a, b \in \alpha$, if $a \leq b$ then $[b, \infty) \subseteq [a, \infty)$.
Signature:  : Antitone (Ici : α → Set α)

6. [THEOREM] Subset condition for closed interval within left-infinite right-closed interval: $[a₁, b₁] \subseteq (-\infty, b₂] \leftrightarrow b₁ \leq b₂$
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Set.Basic
Name: Set.Icc_subset_Iic_iff
Description: For elements $a₁, b₁, a₂, b₂$ in a preorder $\alpha$ with $a₁ \leq b₁$, the closed interval $[a₁, b₁]$ is a subset of the left-infinite right-closed interval $(-\infty, b₂]$ if and only if $b₁ \leq b₂$.
Signature:  (h₁ : a₁ ≤ b₁) : Icc a₁ b₁ ⊆ Iic b₂ ↔ b₁ ≤ b₂
