1. [THEOREM] Fundamental Theorem of Calculus for Right-Differentiable Functions: $\int_a^b f' = f(b) - f(a)$
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.FundThmCalculus
Name: intervalIntegral.integral_eq_sub_of_hasDeriv_right_of_le
Description: Let $a \leq b$ be real numbers, and let $f \colon [a, b] \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function on the closed interval $[a, b]$. Suppose that for every $x \in (a, b)$, the function $f$ has a right derivative $f'(x)$ within the right neighborhood $(x, \infty)$, and that $f'$ is integrable on $[a, b]$. Then:
