1. [THEOREM] Integral of Antitone Function on Interval Bounded by Sum Over Integer Points
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SumIntegralComparisons
Name: AntitoneOn.integral_le_sum_Ico
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be an antitone (decreasing) function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, where $a \leq b$. Then the integral of $f$ over $[a, b]$ is bounded above by the sum of $f$ evaluated at the integer points in the half-open interval $[a, b)$, i.e.,
\[
\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, dx \leq \sum_{x \in [a, b) \cap \mathbb{Z}} f(x).
\]
Signature:  (hab : a ≤ b) (hf : AntitoneOn f (Set.Icc a b)) : (∫ x in a..b, f x) ≤ ∑ x ∈ Finset.Ico a b, f x

2. [THEOREM] Sum of Antitone Function at Right Endpoints is Bounded by Integral over Interval
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SumIntegralComparisons
Name: AntitoneOn.sum_le_integral_Ico
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be an antitone (decreasing) function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, where $a \leq b$. Then the sum of the values of $f$ at the right endpoints of the integer subintervals in $[a, b)$, i.e., $f(i+1)$ for each integer $i$ in the half-open interval $[a, b)$, is less than or equal to the integral of $f$ over $[a, b]$. Mathematically, this is expressed as:
\[
\sum_{i \in [a, b) \cap \mathbb{Z}} f(i+1) \leq \int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, dx.
\]
Signature:  (hab : a ≤ b) (hf : AntitoneOn f (Set.Icc a b)) : (∑ i ∈ Finset.Ico a b, f (i + 1 : ℕ)) ≤ ∫ x in a..b, f x

3. [THEOREM] Integral of Antitone Function Bounded by Sum Over Integer Points
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SumIntegralComparisons
Name: AntitoneOn.integral_le_sum
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be an antitone (decreasing) function on the interval $[x_0, x_0 + a]$. Then the integral of $f$ over $[x_0, x_0 + a]$ is bounded above by the sum of $f$ evaluated at the integer points $x_0, x_0 + 1, \ldots, x_0 + (a - 1)$, i.e.,
\[
\int_{x_0}^{x_0 + a} f(x) \, dx \leq \sum_{i=0}^{a-1} f(x_0 + i).
\]
Signature:  (hf : AntitoneOn f (Icc x₀ (x₀ + a))) : (∫ x in x₀..x₀ + a, f x) ≤ ∑ i ∈ Finset.range a, f (x₀ + i)

1. [THEOREM] Commutative Additivity of Interval Integrals: $\int_a^b f + \int_c^d f = \int_a^d f + \int_c^b f$
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.integral_interval_add_interval_comm
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to E$ be a function and $\mu$ a measure on $\mathbb{R}$. If $f$ is interval integrable on $[a,b]$, $[c,d]$, and $[a,c]$, then the sum of the integrals over $[a,b]$ and $[c,d]$ equals the sum of the integrals over $[a,d]$ and $[c,b]$, i.e.,
\[
\int_a^b f(x) \, d\mu + \int_c^d f(x) \, d\mu = \int_a^d f(x) \, d\mu + \int_c^b f(x) \, d\mu.
\]
Signature:  (hab : IntervalIntegrable f μ a b) (hcd : IntervalIntegrable f μ c d) (hac : IntervalIntegrable f μ a c) :
  ((∫ x in a..b, f x ∂μ) + ∫ x in c..d, f x ∂μ) = (∫ x in a..d, f x ∂μ) + ∫ x in c..b, f x ∂μ

2. [THEOREM] Additivity of Interval Integrals: $\int_a^b f + \int_b^c f = \int_a^c f$
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.integral_add_adjacent_intervals
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to E$ be a function and $\mu$ a measure on $\mathbb{R}$. If $f$ is interval integrable on $[a,b]$ and $[b,c]$, then the sum of the integrals over $[a,b]$ and $[b,c]$ equals the integral over $[a,c]$, i.e.,
