More generally:
- $\sum x \in s, f x$ denotes the sum of $f x$ for $x$ ranging over the finite set $s$ (either a `Finset` or a `Set` with a `Fintype` instance).
- $\sum x \in s \text{ with } p x, f x$ denotes the sum of $f x$ for $x$ in $s$ satisfying the predicate $p$.
- $\sum (x \in s) (y \in t), f x y$ denotes the sum of $f x y$ over the product set $s \times t$.

This notation supports destructuring patterns, such as $\sum \langle x, y \rangle \in s \times t, f x y$.
