1. [THEOREM] Constant Functions are Antitone on Any Subset
Module: Mathlib.Order.Monotone.Basic
Name: antitoneOn_const
Description: For any constant function $f \colon \alpha \to \beta$ between two preorders, where $f(x) = c$ for some fixed $c \in \beta$, and for any subset $s \subseteq \alpha$, the function $f$ is antitone on $s$. That is, for all $a, b \in s$, if $a \leq b$, then $c \leq c$.
Signature:  [Preorder α] [Preorder β] {c : β} {s : Set α} : AntitoneOn (fun _ : α ↦ c) s

2. [THEOREM] Antitonicity Condition for Prepended Vectors: $\text{vecCons}(a, f)$ is antitone $\iff$ $f(0) \leq a$ and $f$ is antitone
Module: Mathlib.Order.Fin.Tuple
Name: antitone_vecCons
Description: For a function $f : \text{Fin}(n+1) \to \alpha$ and an element $a \in \alpha$ in a preordered type $\alpha$, the vector $\text{vecCons}(a, f)$ is antitone if and only if $f(0) \leq a$ and $f$ itself is antitone.
Signature:  : Antitone (vecCons a f) ↔ f 0 ≤ a ∧ Antitone f

1. [THEOREM] Closed Interval as Insertion of Right Endpoint into Closed-Open Interval: $\text{Icc}(a, b) = \text{Ico}(a, b) \cup \{b\}$
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Finset.Basic
Name: Finset.Icc_eq_cons_Ico
Description: For any elements $a$ and $b$ in a locally finite order $\alpha$ with $a \leq b$, the closed interval $[a, b]$ can be constructed by inserting the right endpoint $b$ into the closed-open interval $[a, b)$. Formally, $\text{Icc}(a, b) = \text{Ico}(a, b).\text{cons}(b, \text{right\_not\_mem\_Ico})$, where $\text{right\_not\_mem\_Ico}$ is the proof that $b \notin \text{Ico}(a, b)$.
Signature:  (h : a ≤ b) : Icc a b = (Ico a b).cons b right_not_mem_Ico

2. [THEOREM] Image of $[a, \infty)$ under an antitone function is contained in $(-\infty, f(a)]$
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Set.Image
Name: AntitoneOn.mapsTo_Ici
Description: For a function $f$ that is antitone (decreasing) on the left-closed right-infinite interval $[a, \infty)$, the image of $[a, \infty)$ under $f$ is contained in the left-infinite right-closed interval $(-\infty, f(a)]$.
Signature:  (h : AntitoneOn f (Ici a)) : MapsTo f (Ici a) (Iic (f a))

3. [THEOREM] Image of $[a, \infty)$ under antitone function is contained in $(-\infty, f(a)]$
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Set.Image
Name: AntitoneOn.image_Ici_subset
Description: For a function $f$ that is antitone (decreasing) on the interval $[a, \infty)$, the image of $[a, \infty)$ under $f$ is contained in the interval $(-\infty, f(a)]$.
Signature:  (h : AntitoneOn f (Ici a)) : f '' Ici a ⊆ Iic (f a)

4. [THEOREM] Antitonicity of the $[a, \infty)$ Interval Construction
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Set.Monotone
Name: antitone_Ici
Description: For any preorder $\alpha$, the function that maps each element $a \in \alpha$ to the left-closed right-infinite interval $[a, \infty)$ is antitone. That is, for any $a, b \in \alpha$, if $a \leq b$ then $[b, \infty) \subseteq [a, \infty)$.
Signature:  : Antitone (Ici : α → Set α)

5. [THEOREM] Subset condition for closed interval within left-infinite right-closed interval: $[a₁, b₁] \subseteq (-\infty, b₂] \leftrightarrow b₁ \leq b₂$
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Set.Basic
Name: Set.Icc_subset_Iic_iff
Description: For elements $a₁, b₁, a₂, b₂$ in a preorder $\alpha$ with $a₁ \leq b₁$, the closed interval $[a₁, b₁]$ is a subset of the left-infinite right-closed interval $(-\infty, b₂]$ if and only if $b₁ \leq b₂$.
Signature:  (h₁ : a₁ ≤ b₁) : Icc a₁ b₁ ⊆ Iic b₂ ↔ b₁ ≤ b₂

