The function \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) is continuous on the closed interval \([1,10000]\); hence it is integrable there. For any integrable function on an interval, the integral is additive over adjacent sub‑intervals: for \(a\le b\le c\),

\[
\int_{a}^{c} f(x)\,dx = \int_{a}^{b} f(x)\,dx + \int_{b}^{c} f(x)\,dx .
\]

Taking \(a=1\), \(b=2\) and \(c=10000\) yields exactly the equality stated in the theorem. Therefore the theorem is mathematically correct and can be proved using the standard additivity property of the definite integral in Lean (e.g., `integral_add_adjacent` or `integral_interval_add`). No logical errors are present.
