<justification>
The hypothesis asserts that for every \(x\) in the interval \([1,2]\) we have  

\[
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \le \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}} .
\]

Since the square‑root function is increasing on \([0,\infty)\), we have \(\sqrt{x}\le\sqrt{2}\) for all \(x\in[1,2]\). Taking reciprocals (which reverses the inequality because the numbers are positive) yields exactly the stated pointwise inequality, so the premise is true.

The conclusion is the integral inequality  

\[
\int_{1}^{2}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,dx \;\le\; \int_{1}^{2}\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx .
\]

The integrand on the left is the constant function \(c(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\), while the integrand on the right is the function \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\). By the hypothesis we have \(c(x)\le f(x)\) for every \(x\in[1,2]\). Both functions are measurable and non‑negative, so the monotonicity property of the Lebesgue (or Riemann) integral gives the desired inequality of the integrals. Hence the theorem follows directly from the pointwise bound.

Therefore the statement is mathematically correct and can be proved from the given premise.
</justification>
