1. [THEOREM] Interval Integrability of Reciprocal of Nonzero Continuous Functions
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Integrals
Name: intervalIntegral.intervalIntegrable_one_div
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function on the closed interval $[\min(a, b), \max(a, b)]$ such that $f(x) \neq 0$ for all $x \in [a, b]$. Then the function $x \mapsto 1 / f(x)$ is interval integrable on $[a, b]$ with respect to any measure $\mu$.
Signature:  (h : ∀ x : ℝ, x ∈ [[a, b]] → f x ≠ 0) (hf : ContinuousOn f [[a, b]]) : IntervalIntegrable (fun x => 1 / f x) μ a b

2. [THEOREM] Interval Integral Equals Scaled Integral Over Union Interval: $\int_a^b f = \pm \int_{\text{Ι}(a,b)} f$
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.intervalIntegral_eq_integral_uIoc
Description: For any real numbers $a$ and $b$ and any measurable function $f : \mathbb{R} \to E$, the interval integral $\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, d\mu$ equals the integral of $f$ over the interval $\text{Ι}(a,b) = (\min(a,b), \max(a,b)]$, scaled by $1$ if $a \leq b$ and by $-1$ otherwise. That is,
\[ \int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, d\mu = \begin{cases}
\int_{\text{Ι}(a,b)} f(x) \, d\mu & \text{if } a \leq b \\
-\int_{\text{Ι}(a,b)} f(x) \, d\mu & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases} \]
Signature:  (f : ℝ → E) (a b : ℝ) (μ : Measure ℝ) : ∫ x in a..b, f x ∂μ = (if a ≤ b then 1 else -1 : ℝ) • ∫ x in Ι a b, f x ∂μ
