   f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\qquad (x\in\mathbb{R}).
   \]

   The statement to prove is  

   \[
   \int_{1}^{10000} f = \int_{1}^{2} f + \int_{2}^{10000} f .
   \]

   This is exactly the additivity of interval integrals for the adjacent
   intervals \([1,2]\) and \([2,10000]\).

2. To use the theorem  
   `intervalIntegral.integral_add_adjacent_intervals`,
   we must show that \(f\) is *interval integrable* on \([1,2]\) and on
   \([2,10000]\).

3. The function \(x\mapsto\sqrt{x}\) is continuous on \([0,\infty)\); hence it
   is continuous on the closed intervals \([1,2]\) and \([2,10000]\).
   Moreover, for every \(x>0\) we have \(\sqrt{x}>0\), so \(\sqrt{x}\neq0\)
   on those intervals.  
