[2026-09-05 13:10:20,722][src.models.HILBERTWorker][INFO] - **Solution Explanation**

We have to show  

\[
\int_{1}^{2}\frac1{\sqrt 2}\;dx\;\le\;
\int_{1}^{2}\frac1{\sqrt x}\;dx
\]

under the hypothesis  

\[
\forall x\in[1,2],\qquad \frac1{\sqrt 2}\le\frac1{\sqrt x}.
\]

The proof follows the usual monotonicity of the (interval) integral:

* the constant function \(c:=\frac1{\sqrt 2}\) and the function
  \(g(x):=\frac1{\sqrt x}\) are **interval‑integrable** on \([1,2]\);
* the pointwise inequality gives an **almost‑everywhere** inequality on the
  integration set;
* the lemma `intervalIntegral.integral_le_integral_of_le_ae` turns the
  a.e. inequality into the desired inequality of integrals.

All steps are written explicitly in Lean.

