The function \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) is positive and decreasing on \([1,10000]\); this is exactly the content of the hypothesis `h_antitone`.  

For a decreasing function \(f\) we have the standard integral–sum inequality  

\[
\sum_{k=2}^{n} f(k)\;\le\; \int_{1}^{n} f(x)\,dx ,
\]

because on each interval \([k-1,k]\) the rectangle of height \(f(k)\) lies below the graph of \(f\).  

Taking \(n=10000\) and \(f(x)=1/\sqrt{x}\) gives  

\[
\sum_{i\in\text{Finset.Ico }1\;10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{i+1}}
   =\sum_{k=2}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}
   \le \int_{1}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx .
\]

Hence the inequality asserted in the theorem follows directly from the monotonicity hypothesis, and no contradictory assumptions are present. Therefore the theorem statement is mathematically correct.
