Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.Lebesgue
Name: MeasureTheory.term∫⁻_,_∂_
Description: The notation `∫⁻ x, f x ∂μ` represents the lower Lebesgue integral of an extended non-negative real-valued function `f : α → ℝ≥0∞` with respect to a measure `μ` on `α`. 

Alternative forms include:
- `∫⁻ x, f x` for integration with respect to the canonical measure `volume`
- `∫⁻ x in s, f x ∂μ` for integration over a set `s` with respect to `μ` (defined as `∫⁻ x, f x ∂(μ.restrict s)`)
- `∫⁻ x in s, f x` for integration over a set `s` with respect to `volume`
Signature:  : Lean.ParserDescr✝

5. [DEFINITION] Notation for lower Lebesgue integral of extended non-negative real-valued functions
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.Lebesgue
Name: MeasureTheory.term∫⁻_,_.delab_app.MeasureTheory.lintegral
Description: The notation `∫⁻ x, f x` represents the lower Lebesgue integral of an extended non-negative real-valued function `f : α → ℝ≥0∞` with respect to the canonical measure `volume` on the type `α`. 

This is a special case of the more general notation `∫⁻ x, f x ∂μ` which represents integration with respect to an arbitrary measure `μ`. The notation `∫⁻ x in s, f x` represents integration over a set `s` with respect to the canonical measure.
Signature:  : Delab✝

