<justification>
The function \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) is continuous on the closed interval \([1,10000]\); hence it is integrable on any sub‑interval of this domain.  
For an integrable function the definite integral is additive over adjacent intervals:

\[
\int_{a}^{c} f = \int_{a}^{b} f + \int_{b}^{c} f \qquad (a<b<c).
\]

Taking \(a=1\), \(b=2\) and \(c=10000\) yields exactly the claimed equality

\[
\left(\int_{1}^{2}\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx\right) + 
\left(\int_{2}^{10000}\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx\right) =
\int_{1}^{10000}\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx .
\]

