6. [THEOREM] Difference of Closed and Closed-Open Interval Finsets: $\text{Icc}(a,b) \setminus \text{Ico}(a,b) = \{b\}$
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Finset.Basic
Name: Finset.Icc_diff_Ico_self
Description: For any elements $a$ and $b$ in a locally finite order $\alpha$ with $a \leq b$, the set difference between the finsets $\text{Icc}(a, b)$ and $\text{Ico}(a, b)$ is the singleton finset $\{b\}$. In other words, $\text{Icc}(a, b) \setminus \text{Ico}(a, b) = \{b\}$.
Signature:  (h : a ≤ b) : Icc a b \ Ico a b = { b }

7. [THEOREM] Difference of Closed and Open Interval Finsets Yields Endpoints: $\text{Icc}(a,b) \setminus \text{Ioo}(a,b) = \{a,b\}$
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Finset.Basic
Name: Finset.Icc_diff_Ioo_self
Description: For any elements $a$ and $b$ in a locally finite order $\alpha$ with $a \leq b$, the set difference between the closed interval finset $\text{Icc}(a, b)$ and the open interval finset $\text{Ioo}(a, b)$ is the two-element finset $\{a, b\}$. In other words, $\text{Icc}(a, b) \setminus \text{Ioo}(a, b) = \{a, b\}$.
Signature:  (h : a ≤ b) : Icc a b \ Ioo a b = { a, b }

