\[ \int_{x \in a..b} c \, d\mu = \big(\mu(\text{Ioc}(a,b))_{\text{toReal}} - \mu(\text{Ioc}(b,a))_{\text{toReal}}\big) \cdot c, \]
where $\text{Ioc}(a,b)$ denotes the half-open interval $(a, b]$.
Signature:  [CompleteSpace E] (c : E) : ∫ _ in a..b, c ∂μ = ((μ <| Ioc a b).toReal - (μ <| Ioc b a).toReal) • c

5. [THEOREM] Scaling Property of Interval Integral with Respect to Measure Scaling: $\int_{a}^{b} f \, d(c \cdot \mu) = c_{\text{real}} \cdot \int_{a}^{b} f \, d\mu$
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.integral_smul_measure
Description: For any non-negative extended real number $c \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \cup \{\infty\}$ and any measure $\mu$ on $\mathbb{R}$, the integral of a function $f$ over the interval $[a, b]$ with respect to the scaled measure $c \cdot \mu$ is equal to the real part of $c$ multiplied by the integral of $f$ over $[a, b]$ with respect to $\mu$. That is,
\[ \int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, d(c \cdot \mu) = c_{\text{real}} \cdot \int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, d\mu, \]
where $c_{\text{real}}$ denotes the finite part of $c$ (i.e., $c_{\text{real}} = c$ if $c < \infty$ and $0$ otherwise).
Signature:  (c : ℝ≥0∞) : ∫ x in a..b, f x ∂c • μ = c.toReal • ∫ x in a..b, f x ∂μ

