<justification>
The function \(f(x)=\frac1{\sqrt{x}}\) is decreasing on \([1,10000]\) (premise `h_antitone`). Hence for every \(x\in[1,2]\) we have \(f(x)\ge f(2)=\frac1{\sqrt2}\). Integrating this inequality over \([1,2]\) yields  

\[
\int_{1}^{2}\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx \;>\; \int_{1}^{2}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,dx
      = (2-1)\cdot\frac1{\sqrt2}
      = \frac1{\sqrt2}.
\]

Thus the conclusion \(\displaystyle\int_{1}^{2}\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx > \frac1{\sqrt2}\) follows directly from the antitone hypothesis (together with the obvious positivity of the integrand). The other supplied hypotheses (`h_integral_eq`, `h_le`, `h_pos`, `h_sum_strict`, `h_int_big_strict`) are not needed for this particular inequality, but they do not introduce any contradiction. Consequently the theorem statement is mathematically correct and can be proved from the given premises.
</justification>
