1. [THEOREM] Positivity of Integral for Everywhere Positive Functions: $\int_a^b f(x) dx > 0$ when $f>0$ and $a<b$
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.intervalIntegral_pos_of_pos
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be a strictly positive function (i.e., $f(x) > 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$) that is interval integrable with respect to the Lebesgue measure on $[a,b]$. If $a < b$, then the integral $\int_a^b f(x) \, dx$ is strictly positive.
Signature:  {f : ℝ → ℝ} {a b : ℝ} (hfi : IntervalIntegrable f MeasureSpace.volume a b) (hpos : ∀ x, 0 < f x) (hab : a < b) :
  0 < ∫ x in a..b, f x

2. [THEOREM] Positivity of Integral for Positive Functions on Open Interval
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.intervalIntegral_pos_of_pos_on
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be an interval integrable function with respect to the Lebesgue measure on $[a,b]$. If $f$ is strictly positive on the open interval $(a,b)$ and $a < b$, then the integral $\int_a^b f(x) \, dx$ is strictly positive.
Signature:  {f : ℝ → ℝ} {a b : ℝ} (hfi : IntervalIntegrable f volume a b) (hpos : ∀ x : ℝ, x ∈ Ioo a b → 0 < f x) (hab : a < b) :
  0 < ∫ x : ℝ in a..b, f x

3. [THEOREM] Sum of Antitone Function at Right Endpoints is Bounded by Integral over Interval
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SumIntegralComparisons
Name: AntitoneOn.sum_le_integral_Ico
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be an antitone (decreasing) function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, where $a \leq b$. Then the sum of the values of $f$ at the right endpoints of the integer subintervals in $[a, b)$, i.e., $f(i+1)$ for each integer $i$ in the half-open interval $[a, b)$, is less than or equal to the integral of $f$ over $[a, b]$. Mathematically, this is expressed as:
\[
\sum_{i \in [a, b) \cap \mathbb{Z}} f(i+1) \leq \int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, dx.
\]
Signature:  (hab : a ≤ b) (hf : AntitoneOn f (Set.Icc a b)) : (∑ i ∈ Finset.Ico a b, f (i + 1 : ℕ)) ≤ ∫ x in a..b, f x
