The integral of \(x \mapsto \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) on \([1,2]\) is elementary.  
An antiderivative is \(F(x)=2\sqrt{x}\) because \(\frac{d}{dx}(2\sqrt{x}) = \frac{2}{2\sqrt{x}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\).  
Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus,

\[
\int_{1}^{2} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx = F(2)-F(1)=2\sqrt{2}-2\sqrt{1}=2\sqrt{2}-2.
\]

The right‑hand side of the theorem statement is exactly \((2 : \mathbb{R}) * \text{Real.sqrt } 2 - 2\), which equals \(2\sqrt{2}-2\). Hence the equality holds, making the theorem mathematically correct.
